    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eSoy and vinegar are the backbone, so buy them once and keep them.\u003c/strong\u003e A single bottle of each seasons this batch and a dozen after it. This is a recipe that spends almost nothing on the flavor and most of its money on the chicken.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBay leaves live in the pantry for a year.\u003c/strong\u003e One small jar covers this and every braise you make until next summer, so do not skip them for the cost.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy the black beans by the can and drain them well.\u003c/strong\u003e A couple of 15 ounce cans covers all 14 burritos, and draining keeps the wrap from going soggy in the fridge."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Make the adobo braise: combine the soy sauce, white vinegar, brown sugar, smashed garlic, bay leaves, and black pepper in a pot.",
                    "Add the chicken breast, bring to a simmer, and cook until the chicken reaches 165F and the liquid has reduced to a glaze. Pull out the bay leaves, then shred or chop the chicken back into the glaze.",
                    "Cook 1.25 cups of dry rice per the package and set aside.",
                    "Warm the drained black beans through.",
                    "Make the fresh salsa: toss the quartered cherry tomatoes, diced red onion, chopped cilantro, and lime juice together.",
                    "Warm the tortillas so they roll cleanly. Lay down rice, then the adobo chicken, then black beans, and top with the tomato and onion salsa before you wrap."
